import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { ListboxHero } from "../../demos/listbox/hero.rx"
import { ListboxMultiple } from "../../demos/listbox/multiple.rx"
import { ListboxGrouped } from "../../demos/listbox/grouped.rx"
import { ListboxHorizontal } from "../../demos/listbox/horizontal.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Listbox" subtitle="An always-visible list of options to select one or several from.">
    <Demo source="listbox/hero"><ListboxHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="listbox/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      Base UI has no listbox, so this family follows the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices listbox pattern, as React
      Aria's ListBox and Ark UI's Listbox do. The list is the same one Select opens in its popup, without the
      trigger or the popup.
    </p>
    <p>
      The native listbox is a <code>{"<select multiple>"}</code> or <code>{"<select size>"}</code>, and it isn't
      what this family is built on. Its options can be styled only with <code>appearance: base-select</code>,
      which isn't shipped in Firefox. Without it, WebKit lays the options out itself and ignores their padding, and
      Chromium and Firefox paint the option just pressed, while the list has focus, in their own highlight colour
      rather than the stylesheet's <code>:checked</code> rule. A click in a multiple list replaces the selection instead of toggling it, and there is
      no horizontal list. The tests in{" "}
      <code>src/listbox/native-select.browser.test.ts</code> show each of these in the engines it applies to.{" "}
      <code>focusgroup</code>, which would give the arrow keys to any element, is Chrome only.
    </p>
    <p>What the browser still provides:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Real focus on the options. The focused option matches <code>:focus-visible</code>, and the browser
        scrolls it into view in a list that scrolls.
      </li>
      <li>Form submission and reset, through hidden inputs the root renders beside the list.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The library adds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The roles: a <code>listbox</code> with <code>aria-orientation</code>, and{" "}
        <code>aria-multiselectable</code> when <code>multiple</code>, of <code>option</code>s carrying{" "}
        <code>aria-selected</code> and <code>aria-disabled</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        One Tab stop for the whole list. <kbd>Tab</kbd> enters on the selected option (the first selected, with{" "}
        <code>multiple</code>), else on the option focused last, else on the first, and leaves in one step.
      </li>
      <li>
        The arrow keys along the orientation, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>, skipping disabled options, and
        typeahead: typing focuses the next option whose label starts with what was typed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Selection: a click, <kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd> selects the focused option. The multiple-selection
        keys are under <a href="#multiple">Multiple selection</a>.
      </li>
      <li>The form value and reset, and the wiring to an enclosing FieldRoot.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There is no Separator part: a listbox may contain only options and groups. Draw a line between groups in
        CSS, as the grouped demo does. There is no Label part either; label the root with{" "}
        <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code>, or put it in a FieldRoot. Options have no{" "}
        <code>data-highlighted</code>: the focused option is the highlighted one, and <code>:focus-visible</code>{" "}
        styles it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="labelling">Labelling it</h3>
    <p>
      The first demo puts the list in a <code>FieldRoot</code>, whose <code>FieldLabel</code> names it through{" "}
      <code>aria-labelledby</code> (a <code>{"<label for>"}</code> can't name a list). Outside a field, give{" "}
      <code>ListboxRoot</code> an <code>aria-label</code>, as the demos below do.
    </p>

    <h3 id="multiple">Multiple selection</h3>
    <p>
      With <code>multiple</code>, the value is a list of strings, and selecting is independent of focus: the arrow
      keys move focus without changing the selection. <kbd>Space</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and a click toggle an
      option. <kbd>Shift</kbd>+arrow moves focus and toggles the option it reaches. <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click and{" "}
      <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> add every option from the anchor (the option last toggled) to this one.{" "}
      <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Home</kbd> or <kbd>End</kbd> adds every option from the focused one to
      the first or last and moves there. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> on a Mac)
      selects every enabled option, and clears them when all are already selected. Disabled options are left out of
      every range.
    </p>
    <p>
      Give <code>ListboxItemIndicator</code> the <code>keepMounted</code> prop to draw a box on every option, and
      fill it from <code>data-selected</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo source="listbox/multiple"><ListboxMultiple /></Demo>

    <h3 id="groups">Groups</h3>
    <p>
      <code>ListboxGroup</code> gives related options <code>role="group"</code>, named by the{" "}
      <code>ListboxGroupLabel</code> inside it. The arrow keys run through the groups as one list. Give the root a
      height and <code>overflow-y: auto</code> for a list that scrolls; the focused option is scrolled into view.
    </p>
    <Demo source="listbox/grouped"><ListboxGrouped /></Demo>

    <h3 id="orientation">Orientation and selection following focus</h3>
    <p>
      <code>orientation="horizontal"</code> moves focus with <kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd>, and lays nothing
      out: the row is the stylesheet's. <code>loop</code> wraps the arrow keys around at the ends. With{" "}
      <code>selectionFollowsFocus</code>, moving focus with the keyboard selects the option it reaches, as in a
      group of radios; <kbd>Tab</kbd> entering the list doesn't change it. It applies to single selection only.
    </p>
    <Demo source="listbox/horizontal"><ListboxHorizontal /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling the value</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> to hold the selection yourself: a string, or{" "}
      <code>null</code> for nothing selected, or a list of strings with <code>multiple</code>. This differs from
      Select, whose multiple value is a separate <code>values</code> prop. The listbox calls{" "}
      <code>onValueChange</code> in a change of its own, so a handler that saves to a server, or writes something a
      loading question reads, doesn't hold the list: with an optimistic <code>guess</code>, the new selection shows
      at once and a failed save puts the old one back.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forms">In a form</h3>
    <p>
      Give the root a <code>name</code>, or put it in a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, and the selected value is
      submitted with its form; with <code>multiple</code>, each value is its own entry. A form reset puts back the
      value the list started with. <code>required</code> makes the form invalid while nothing is selected.{" "}
      <code>disabled</code> on the root, or on an enclosing <code>FieldRoot</code>, takes the list out of the Tab
      order and ignores presses. A <code>disabled</code> option is skipped by the arrow keys and typeahead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Options can come from a server: render the answer as the items. A value chosen before they arrive is
      submitted meanwhile, and becomes the Tab stop once its option is there.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="listbox" />
  </DocsPage>
}
